::GEO_U03_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L07_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.2<br><br>Question:<br>In a parallelogram, which sides are congruent? {
=A. Opposite sides#Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent.
~B. Adjacent sides only#Adjacent sides are not always congruent in every parallelogram.
~C. Diagonals only#Diagonals have other relationships but are not side pairs.
~D. No sides#Parallelograms do have congruent opposite sides.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L07_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.2<br><br>Question:<br>In a parallelogram, which angles are congruent? {
~A. Consecutive angles#Consecutive angles are supplementary, not necessarily congruent.
=B. Opposite angles#Opposite angles of a parallelogram are congruent.
~C. All exterior angles only#Exterior angles are not the standard relationship used here.
~D. No angles#Parallelograms do have congruent opposite angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L07_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.2<br><br>Question:<br>In a parallelogram, consecutive angles are what? {
~A. Always congruent#Opposite angles are congruent; consecutive angles are supplementary.
~B. Always 90&deg;#Only rectangles guarantee right angles.
=C. Supplementary#Consecutive angles in a parallelogram sum to 180&deg;.
~D. Always acute#Consecutive angles can vary but must sum to 180&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L07_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.2<br><br>Question:<br>What do the diagonals of a parallelogram do? {
~A. Stay perpendicular in every case#Perpendicular diagonals are special, not true for every parallelogram.
~B. Have no relationship#There is a defined diagonal relationship.
~C. Create four equilateral triangles#This is not a general parallelogram property.
=D. Bisect each other#Diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L07_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.3<br><br>Question:<br>A trapezoid has bases 8 and 14. What is the midsegment length? {
~A. 6#This subtracts the base lengths. The trapezoid midsegment uses the average of the bases, not the difference.
~B. 22#This adds the bases but does not divide by 2. The midsegment is halfway between the base lengths.
=C. 11#Correct. The trapezoid midsegment length is (8 + 14) / 2 = 11.
~D. 112#This multiplies the bases. The trapezoid midsegment formula uses the average of the base lengths.
}

